      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227407/assets/a66ac2663dadacc0648b5a8993df40edafe8c7c2ee9dddac4ef932203b99dbd7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227407/assets/f5de147e69639b8cfbbe591241603d437650b88d523058df09dd30b60a6482da.png)",
